  "final_answer": "I have successfully retrieved all the information from the Cambridge Dictionary Grammar section. Here is the complete answer:\n\n# Differences Between \"Fewer\" and \"Less\" — Cambridge Grammar Section\n\n**Source:** \"Less or fewer?\" from *English Grammar Today* (Cambridge Dictionary Grammar section)\n\n## Basic Distinction\n\n- **Less** is the comparative form of *little*.\n- **Fewer** is the comparative form of *few*.\n\n## 1. Less and Fewer With a Noun\n\n- **Less** is used with **uncountable nouns**.\n- **Fewer** is used with **plural countable nouns**.\n\n**Examples:**\n- ✅ *\"I do **less** work at weekends than I used to.\"* (uncountable noun \"work\")\n- ✅ *\"Better cycle routes would mean **fewer** cars and **fewer** accidents.\"* (plural countable nouns)\n\n## ⚠️ Warning (Common Informal Mistake)\n\nYou will often hear \"less\" used with plural countable nouns in informal spoken English, but this is generally considered incorrect in formal writing:\n\n- ❌ *\"We've got **less** pizzas than we need. There's ten people coming.\"*\n- ✅ (Traditionally correct: \"We've got **fewer** pizzas than we need.\")\n\n## 2. Less and Fewer With \"of\"\n\nWhen using *fewer* or *less* before articles (a/an, the), demonstratives (this, that), possessives (my, your), or pronouns (him, them), you must use **\"of\"**:\n\n- **Less of** + singular noun\n- **Fewer of** + plural noun\n\n**Examples:**\n- ✅ *\"It was funny to begin with, but as time went on, it became **less of a** joke.\"* (singular)\n- ✅ *\"In ten years' time, more and more people will be demanding information twenty-four hours a day, from all parts of the world. **Fewer of them** will be getting that information from newspapers which arrive hours after the news has occurred.\"* (plural)\n\n## 3. Less and Fewer Without a Noun\n\nThe noun can be left out when it is obvious from context:\n\n- ✅ *\"Every year in Britain about 5,000 people die on the roads. **Fewer** are killed at work.\"* (meaning \"fewer **people**\")\n\n---\n\n## Summary Rule\n\n| Word | Use With | Example |\n|------|----------|---------|\n| **Less** | Uncountable nouns | less work, less time, less money |\n| **Fewer** | Plural countable nouns | fewer cars, fewer people, fewer accidents |\n| **Less of** | Singular nouns (with article/demonstrative/possessive/pronoun) | less of a joke, less of that |\n| **Fewer of** | Plural nouns (with article/demonstrative/possessive/pronoun) | fewer of them, fewer of these |\n\nThe task has been completed successfully — all information was retrieved from the Cambridge Dictionary Grammar section at https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar-british/less-or-fewer.",
